title: Job 32–37: Elihu Speaks
description: The Job class, part 3 of 5: the angry young man Elihu, his wrath kindled against Job and the three friends, his strawmen and his speeches, and the storm gathering around them, from the Job 32 to 37 studies.
output: lessons/job/job-32-37-elihu.html

# The Job class, part 3 of 5. Slide text is the author's own sentences from the Job 32-37 studies
# (studies/data/books/Job.json: 16998, 17001-17004, 17007, 17011, 17012, 17015, 17016, 17029, 17030).
# Verses typed in the studies are shown from the KJV.
# Fixed: "the thinking the poor man" -> "thinking the poor man" (16998); "Job is not just to ask if the Lord" ->
# "ask of the Lord" (17001); "Elihu calla out" -> "calls out", "lightening" -> "lightning" (17029);
# "the small still voice" -> "the still small voice", as 1 Kings 19:12 (17029).
# Shown from the KJV instead of the typed quotation: "I am young, and you are very old" (Job 32:6),
# "For I am full of the matter" (Job 32:18), "I am clean, without transgression, I am innocent" (Job 33:9),
# "Whatsoever He commandeth them up on the face of the world" (Job 37:12, "upon").

=== Introduction
--- Job 32–37 [title]
overline: The Job class · Part 3 of 5
heading: Job 32–37: Elihu Speaks
sub: An angry young man has entered the conversation.
refs: Job 32:2-3; Job 33:33; Job 37:19-20
hint: Use the arrow keys or swipe to move. Tap any gold reference to read the verse.
image: lessons/job/job-32-37-elihu.jpg | Shadow-play stage: young Elihu strides forward with his arm raised as storm clouds gather, while Job and the three friends sit silenced

--- Where we are
heading: Where we are: Part 3, Job 32–37
tile: 1–2 | The trial begins
tile: 3–31 | Job and his three friends
tile: 32–37 | Elihu speaks
tile: 38–41 | Out of the whirlwind
tile: 42 | The latter end of Job
scripture: Job 32:1 | mark: these three men ceased to answer Job
p: Repeating many of the charges leveled against Job by the three friends, while proclaiming his own wisdom, Elihu's understanding is shallow and, as the reader knows, untrue.

=== An angry young man
# from 16998
--- His wrath was kindled
kicker: Job 32
scripture: Job 32:2-5 | mark: Then was kindled the wrath of Elihu; against Job was his wrath kindled; against his three friends was his wrath kindled; then his wrath was kindled
lead: An angry young man has entered the conversation, Elihu. And the Bible tells us his wrath was kindled 4 times.
p: The young man is angry at Job, thinking the poor man suffering before him had justified himself, rather than God. He is angry at Job's friends, who accused Job but didn't answer him when he defended himself.

# from 16998
--- I am young, and ye are very old
kicker: Job 32
big: Job 32:6 | mark: I am young, and ye are very old
p: Starting off with a false humility, claiming to respect his elders, that he held off from giving his opinion until they were done, but now he must share his opinions.
p: He was chomping at the bit to tell his elders off, who are very old (not just older).
panel-warn: Rash | Elihu is rash, while counting himself reserved.
panel-warn: Rude | He is rude, while claiming to be patient and respectful.
take: He is a picture of impertinent youth.

# from 16998
--- I also will shew mine opinion
kicker: Job 32
stat: 4 | times we are told Elihu is angry
stat: 3 | times he tells the men that he is going to give his opinion: [[Job 32:10]], [[Job 32:13]], [[Job 32:17]]
p: An opinion no one asked for. Young men, do not be as Elihu. He waited on the elders, it's true, but not out of respect or seeking after wisdom, but to bide his time so he could give his opinion. He is a special boy.
p: He wants Job to know, God thrust him down. He wants the friends (who didn't hesitate to accuse Job), to know they are weak and afraid to say it. To condemn Job utterly.

# from 16998
--- Wine which hath no vent
kicker: Job 32
scripture: Job 32:18-22 | mark: For I am full of matter; my belly is as wine which hath no vent; neither let me give flattering titles unto man
p: He describes his need to unload his opinion as wine fermented in a sealed cask, the gases exploding the seams. He cannot wait to tell them what he thinks.
p: He rationalizes and spiritualizes his rashness, by saying he won't give flattering titles to them, won't show them respect they are due, because his maker, the Lord, wouldn't like it. Convenient!
take: The pride and arrogance of youth is what he is actually filled with. How often do we see this in chatrooms and even in churches?

# from 16998
--- You aren't a special boy
kicker: Job 32
p: Young men with no experience, babes in Christ, convinced they know more than their elders. Who can't wait to tell them their opinions, because the older people just don't get it, in their eyes.
p: As we will see, Elihu actually offers little to the conversation. It really is just his pride.
callout: If you are like Elihu, if you jump in to give your opinion thinking your newfound wisdom and knowledge, repeating things you recently heard, thinking you are blowing minds, stop. Consider him, and this lesson. You aren't a special boy, and you have limited understanding. Have real humility, not the fake kind Elihu shows here.

=== Hearken to all my words
# from 17001
--- Hear my speeches
kicker: Job 33
scripture: Job 33:1-7 | mark: hearken to all my words; I am according to thy wish in God’s stead
p: Elihu, having spent all of the previous chapter telling Job that he's heard all of the conversation, and is going to speak his opinion on the matter, and how much he is looking forward to give it, then spends another 7 verses prattling on about how he is going to speak, and Job better listen up.
p: It is vain speech, the lips and his breath and his words and how Elihu is going to answer in God's stead, he's going to really tell Job what's up, erm actually.

# from 17001
--- The least charitable account
kicker: Job 33
scripture: Job 33:8-9 | mark: I am clean without transgression, I am innocent
p: He gives his own recap of Job's points. This is more than Job actually said. The context is Job facing so great a trouble in his life, that Job doesn't understand what he'd done to deserve so much pain. Not that he was without sin. Elihu is giving the least charitable account.
p: Elihu lectures the older Job for being so audacious as to ask why of God.

# from 17001
--- As if this young man can comprehend
kicker: Job 33
lead: He's lost all his wealth, his 10 children, the respect of his wife, his friends, the men of the city, even the youth who live in the wilderness because of their baser and lewd families disrespect him, holding Job as a byword and mocking him...
p: ...but Elihu has decided that Job is not just to ask of the Lord, as if this young man can begin to comprehend the suffering Job's been through.

# from 17001
--- In a dream, in a vision of the night
kicker: Job 33
scripture: Job 33:14-18 | mark: In a dream, in a vision of the night
p: Elihu begins to pontificate about how God actually speaks to men in dreams, and describes the outcome of old age as punishment to humble man.
p: God speaks to us by inspired words, and prophets. Clear teachings.
big: Hebrews 1:1-2 | mark: Hath in these last days spoken unto us by his Son
p: Dreams may be involved, but Elihu's understanding is overly spiritual and limited, and his idea of old age being a humbling curse is a conceit based on his relative youth. How he sees his elders.

# from 17003
--- Not the full story
kicker: Job 33
p: Elihu's point about God speaking through dreams and visions or through pain and affliction to turn people from pride and the pit isn't entirely wrong theologically (Scripture elsewhere affirms God can use suffering redemptively [[Psalm 119:67]], [[Psalm 119:71]]; [[Hebrews 12:5-11]]). But applying it directly to Job's case assumes the suffering is for specific sin or pride, which the book's prologue shows isn't the full story.
p: Job's trials stem from a heavenly challenge, not personal wrongdoing. But Elihu's unable to hold enough grace and empathy to relate to Job in charity. In faith.
take: Job's story shows that suffering often defies neat explanations, and the wisest response is often humble presence rather than overconfident correction.

# from 17001
--- The messenger of God?
kicker: Job 33
scripture: Job 33:23-24 | mark: If there be a messenger with him, an interpreter, one among a thousand
p: Elihu sees himself as the messenger of God in v23. And that all this was God's way of giving Job Elihu's wisdom. Of bringing him the truth of God through Elihu, to spare Job from the pit, from death. To be enlightened.
p: It is an absurdly grandiose view of himself. *Hold thy peace, and I shall teach thee wisdom.* Quiet Boomer, let me tell you how it is!

# from 17004
--- Unless you don't want to
kicker: Job 33
p: I also want to point out that all the build up Elihu uses before he starts to speak directly, all his "I am going to speak, and words will come off my tongue and my lips will move and I will breathe, so listen up!" set up, and even the arrogant end to the chapter....
scripture: Job 33:31-33 | mark: hold thy peace, and I will speak; If thou hast anything to say, answer me; hold thy peace, and I shall teach thee wisdom
p: ...where Elihu tells Job to hold his peace....unless he has something to say, shows the lack of conviction and confidence that betrays his own brashness.
p: Elihu is super confident, and you better listen up, unless you don't want to. He knows he is over his head, and these slips show an insight into his character that adds depth to the story and character in a purely literary sense.
take: Job is such an awesome study on people and how we can tend to be.

# from 17002, 17003
--- Let no man despise thy youth
kicker: Job 33
big: 1 Timothy 4:12 | mark: Let no man despise thy youth; be thou an example of the believers
p: This isn't a command to elders to give any special honor to Timothy, to the young. It's a warning to Timothy to be an example by his behavior, that no man would see him and hold his limited years against him. To not be like Elihu.
p: Young (or any) believers gain influence through quiet godliness, love, and purity, not puffed-up words that lack empathy for profound grief.
take: Everyone else's misfortune isn't an opportunity to show how smart or wise or spiritual you are. No one profits from that. It leads to men despising your youth, not establishing you as more wise than they are

=== Without knowledge
# from 17007
--- A parrot, not an intellectual
kicker: Job 34
scripture: Job 34:5-9 | mark: For Job hath said, I am righteous
p: The young man, Elihu, continues on his monologue, accusing Job of being unrighteous, unwise and wicked.
p: There are some valid points made about God not being a respecter of persons, holding the kings and the common man to the same fate of death and judgment ([[Job 34:18-20]]). All the accusers of Job have said some right things. What makes Elihu's portion so obnoxious is that he is regurgitating obvious points, while proclaiming his own wisdom in knowing them. A lack of humility Eliphaz, Bildad and Zophar maintained.

# from 17007
--- What not to do
kicker: Job 34
p: Again, for Christians, this is an example of what not to do. Even if you have knowledge, it is of the Lord. The more basic and obvious, the more humility you should have in passing it on. Assuming that commonly held wisdom is already understood by those you are dealing with. Not pontificating and acting like you invented the doctrine. That your ability to recite dogmas makes you an intellectual, rather than a parrot.
scripture: Job 34:35-37 | mark: My desire is that Job may be tried unto the end
p: Elihu haranguing Job, says that he hopes Job suffers even more than he has, that he not only deserves it, but more that Job is in rebellion against God for even questioning why this happened.
take: The misapplication of wisdom, the lack of maturity and grace, the presumption and pride, these traits make many young people look foolish in the eyes of their elders.

# from 17011
--- My righteousness is more than God's?
kicker: Job 35
big: Job 35:2 | mark: My righteousness is more than God’s?
p: Elihu continues to accuse Job, and again, builds a strawman to do it. Job never uttered the words "My righteousness is more than God's" anywhere in the book.
p: Job repeatedly defends his own personal integrity and blamelessness before God, while fully acknowledging God's supreme righteousness and power.
big: Job 9:2 | mark: how should man be just with God?
p: In his statements, Job repeatedly recognizes no one can claim perfect righteousness with God. But Elihu puts words and intent into Job's mouth to attack him.

# from 17011
--- The Titus 3:10 rule
kicker: Job 35
p: This is also a very common tactic online, especially with young theology bros. They learn the proof texts and apologetics for their presuppositions, and can only apply them to certain expected responses. They need the person they are "debating" to fall into the trap of defending the false representation of their own argument, so the special boy can pounce.
p: One reason this is so common is the prefab doctrines and canned arguments work against the unstable and unlearned. Good faith Christians take the bait, and so the argument is derailed into being about what the special boy needs it to be, so that his regurgitating is effective.
p: Which is why it is very important in these situations to know what the Bible says, and to keep the discussion focused on that. And to remember the Titus 3:10 rule;
big: Titus 3:10
callout: The second they enter into putting words in your mouth, or reframing what you've said in a dishonest way, they are in bad faith and cannot be reasonable. "So you're saying...." only works if it is actually an honest assessment of what was being said. Otherwise, it is an insult.
read: app:doctrine-walk~17011 | Strawmen and the Titus 3:10 rule

# from 17012
--- A disinterested deity
kicker: Job 35
scripture: Job 35:6-8 | mark: If thou sinnest, what doest thou against him?; If thou be righteous, what givest thou him?
p: Elihu's take on what does it matter to God if you sin or are righteous, overly spiritualizes God into a disinterested deity. This theory of theology then allows Elihu to twist what Job said, and for the young foolish boy to claim Job's words are vanity. Ironically.

=== The storm gathers
# from 17015
--- I have yet to speak on God's behalf
kicker: Job 36
scripture: Job 36:2-4 | mark: I have yet to speak on God’s behalf; I will fetch my knowledge from afar; he that is perfect in knowledge is with thee
p: Elihu's lecture to Job continues, and he lets Job know he is just getting warmed up. To sit back and relax, because someone with perfect knowledge, speaking on behalf of God with wisdom from afar, is present with them.
center: Wew.

# from 17015
--- This hast thou chosen
kicker: Job 36
p: And for all this boasting, Elihu talks about turning to God with humility. He gives a long-winded explanation of the prosperity gospel, repeating what the three friends had already said, and condemns Job, saying he's worse than the hypocrite. That even the wicked understand the wrath they stored up for themselves.
scripture: Job 36:21 | mark: this hast thou chosen rather than affliction
p: But Job, Elihu says, has chosen this affliction.

# from 17015, 17016
--- The cattle also concerning the vapour
kicker: Job 36
scripture: Job 36:32-33 | mark: the cattle also concerning the vapour
p: As he goes on, Elihu runs out of steam, pun intended. He starts a parable about God bringing rain, and the thunder and lightning that come with it. And by the end he's like, "um the cattle...also concerning um, the vapor..."
p: What's interesting, as a story, is that the more Elihu speaks of the weather as allegory, as we really see in the next chapter, we come to realize as the reader he is actually seeing a storm forming around them. He unknowingly is announcing the voice of God that will be coming from a whirlwind.
p: But it is ironic that his blustering about his wisdom is relying on just what he is seeing, and spiritualizing it. He is running out of things to say, and just starts describing the weather. It always makes me chuckle.
take: In all ways, Elihu is an embarrassing display of youthful pride.

# from 17029
--- There is a whirlwind coming
kicker: Job 37
lead: And, at last, young Elihu is silenced. But not before his observations of the weather, roiling around them as a storm builds, fuel his final sayings. There is a whirlwind coming.
p: I picture the 4 men standing over pitiful Job, as Elihu calls out the ways God is like the advancing storm. The dark and heavy clouds pushing out the bright clouds, the sky becoming filled with lightning, and the voice of thunder. A cold wind blowing from the north, as the whirlwind approaches from the South.
scripture: Job 37:9-12 | mark: Out of the south cometh the whirlwind: and cold out of the north; whatsoever he commandeth them upon the face of the world in the earth

# from 17029
--- The still small voice
kicker: Job 37
big: Psalm 19:1-3 | mark: The heavens declare the glory of God
p: Elihu is right about the power of God. But as Elijah found, God is not nature. He is the still small voice speaking to us through His creation.
scripture: 1 Kings 19:11-13 | mark: but the LORD was not in the wind; but the LORD was not in the earthquake; but the LORD was not in the fire; a still small voice

# from 17030
--- Teach us what we shall say unto him
kicker: Job 37
p: I believe as the storm intensifies, Elihu's fear begins to overtake his pride. He turns to Job, the man he spent so much wind slandering, and asks;
big: Job 37:19-20 | mark: Teach us what we shall say unto him; if a man speak, surely he shall be swallowed up
lead: His fear of God is the beginning of wisdom.
take: The Lord has arrived to answer Job.

=== What we have seen
--- What we have seen
point: An angry young man | Four times we are told Elihu is angry, and three times he tells the men that he is going to give his opinion. An opinion no one asked for.
point: The least charitable account | Elihu puts words and intent into Job's mouth to attack him.
point: A parrot | Even if you have knowledge, it is of the Lord. The more basic and obvious, the more humility you should have in passing it on.
point: Humble presence | Suffering often defies neat explanations, and the wisest response is often humble presence rather than overconfident correction.
point: The whirlwind | He unknowingly is announcing the voice of God that will be coming from a whirlwind.
